00:02:58.470 --> 00:02:59.050
All right?

00:02:59.050 --> 00:02:59.970
ANTHONY BUSHONG:
Yeah, sounds good.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:00.170
YURI GRINSHTEYN: All right.

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:02.503
So let's start by talking
about service levels and error

00:03:02.503 --> 00:03:03.390
budgets.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:07.710
So this is a kind of famous
line that in some ways,

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:10.270
I think, underpins a lot
of what SRE is all about.

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:13.290
And that is that 100%
is the wrong reliability

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:14.443
target for everything.

00:03:14.443 --> 00:03:15.360
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Yeah.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:16.770
We have a couple of posters.

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:19.280
But I am curious to hear.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.720
So you explain a little
bit about the methodology

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.730
or the understanding
behind that?

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:25.555
Why isn't it a good
target for reliability?

00:03:25.555 --> 00:03:27.180
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Yeah, a good question.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.920
At a very fundamental level--

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.930
because there's no point
in delivering a service

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:32.610
that's 100% reliable.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:36.210
Because your users will never
experience 100% reliability.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:38.910
The methods that they use
to connect and consume

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.370
your service are
not 100% reliable.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.080
And they never will be.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.013
You've all got phones.

00:03:44.013 --> 00:03:45.180
You all use mobile networks.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.340
You all use the Wi-Fi
at the conference.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:50.070
None of that stuff
is 100% reliable.

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:53.160
So what is the point of
running a service 100% reliable

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.840
that users will
never benefit from?

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.230
The second thing is, the
only way you actually

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:03.150
achieve 100% reliability,
is to eliminate all changes.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:04.770
Change is risk.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:07.900
Risk means failure,
means downtime.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.380
So the only way you prevent
any downtime or any performance

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:13.050
issues is by removing
all change, which means

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:14.280
you can never do anything.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:15.690
You can never build anything.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.839
You can never advance the
service in any meaningful way.

00:04:18.839 --> 00:04:21.954
So that's why 100%
is the wrong target.

00:04:21.954 --> 00:04:23.220
OK?

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:27.080
So then if 100% percent
is the wrong target,

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.950
what is the right target?

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:30.800
What is the Service
Level Objective

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:32.690
that your service
should deliver on?

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:37.490
And conversely, what do you
do with that gap between 100%

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:38.700
and your actual SLO, right?

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:40.490
Then that's the error budget.

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:42.500
And the big thing
that I think is often

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:44.870
lost in this
conversation of SLOs

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.630
and error budgets is the idea
that error budgets actually

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:49.880
allow you to increase velocity.

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:53.090
Service Level Objectives, and
strictly adhering to them,

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:56.080
is not just a way to prevent
developers from doing stuff.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:58.580
If at the end of the month, or
at the end of the SLO window,

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:00.410
whatever it may
be, yet you still

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:02.300
have error budget
available, that's

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:04.970
actually an opportunity for your
developers to do more stuff.

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:07.280
It's room to experiment.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:10.550
They can experiment with
the code, experiment

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:13.460
with flags and
features, experiment

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:15.980
with the infrastructure,
and config management.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:20.780
All of that is enabled by having
development and operations,

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:23.240
or Site Reliability
Engineering, align

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:27.330
on a common principle of
operating from an error budget.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:28.820
So then the next
obvious question

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:30.320
is, all right, that
all sounds good.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:31.070
We all agree.

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:32.232
SLOs are the way to go.

00:05:32.232 --> 00:05:34.190
But we're here today to
talk about putting them

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:37.460
into practice, especially
with Stackdriver.

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:38.940
So how do we do this?

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:41.660
Well, what we want to do
is to actually configure

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:44.420
monitoring for
SLOs in Stackdriver

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:47.360
so that we can actually
measure and make sure

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:49.910
that we are, in fact,
delivering on the promises

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:53.210
that we made to our users
in the Service Level

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:54.990
Objective, or the SLO.

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:59.240
And then we want an alert
on both SLO violations,

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:01.130
but also increases
in error budget

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:04.700
burn rate, that let us know when
an outage or an SLO violation

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:06.320
might actually be pending.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:08.560
So what is that
going to look like?

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:12.940
I'm going to stop the
slide deck for a second.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:17.120
And here, we're going to get
into some of the new stuff that

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:20.180
was actually announced,
basically, in a letter today.

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:21.300
You saw the keynote.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:24.240
You saw Anthos as one
of the announcements.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.830
One of the key components
is the Cloud Service Mesh,

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:28.580
or the Anthos Service Mesh.

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:30.080
What this is going
to allow us to do

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:33.050
is actually expose all
of the services that

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:36.080
are now running in our
project for any given service.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:38.390
For example, this
product-page service,

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:41.310
I can get details for it.

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:44.030
Let's see how good the--
speaking a reliable Wi-Fis.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:45.690
That's not bad.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:48.650
We can go to the-- ah,
well, there you go.

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:50.870
We are having a reliability
issue with our service

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:53.990
reliability monitoring.

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:55.730
We can get into the service.

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:57.800
We can understand what's
actually going on there.

00:06:57.800 --> 00:06:59.420
We can set SLOs.

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:02.750
And from there, we can actually
set a learning policies

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:06.410
on SLO violations that will
take us into Stackdriver.

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:10.010
So now, we have the ability to
monitor service health directly

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:12.140
within the Cloud Console.

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:15.740
We have the ability to set
SLOs against those services,

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:20.090
and actually configure alerting
and notifications on not just

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:23.450
network thresholds the way that
alerting policies have enabled

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:26.340
us to do for years
at this point,

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:29.120
but actual SLO
violations, all right.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:31.820
So that's really kind of
the first SRE principle

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:36.350
that we want to cover, which
is all about operating off

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:40.520
SLOs, not just metric
thresholds, and then

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:42.548
configure alerting for
those in Stackdriver.

00:07:42.548 --> 00:07:44.090
ANTHONY BUSHONG:
And ultimately, this

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:46.160
brings us closer to a
user-first mindset, right?

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.890
Because ultimately, the
user isn't seeing directly

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:49.760
CP utilization.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:51.343
But they are certainly
seeing whatever

00:07:51.343 --> 00:07:53.385
you are defining as your
service-level objective.

00:07:53.385 --> 00:07:54.680
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah, exactly.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:57.650
All right, so let's move on to
our second principle, which is

00:07:57.650 --> 00:07:59.660
all about observability, right?

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:01.970
So what is observability?

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:07.363
Well, it's just
monitoring repackaged.

00:08:07.363 --> 00:08:09.530
I'm quoting someone here
who is a lot smarter than I

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:11.540
am and knows a lot
more about this.

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:13.820
Cindy literally wrote
the book on monitoring

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:15.200
distributed systems.

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:16.940
If you haven't read
it, you should.

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:18.070
It's really good.

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:20.150
And she like, I said,
knows this stuff a lot

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:22.770
more than either one of us do.

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:25.130
But essentially, observability
is the combination

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:31.120
of what we often refer to as the
golden signals, metrics, logs,

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:33.600
and traces.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:36.710
So a logical question might be,
well, why do we need all that?

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:38.360
What does all of that do?

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:40.460
Well, I'll start by
talking about metrics.

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:43.549
Metrics are a way
to really understand

00:08:43.549 --> 00:08:46.190
the health of our system, and
the health of the components

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:48.770
in our system, over time.

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:50.930
Because they represent
the movement of specific

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:53.690
values, again,
against a time scale.

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:55.610
And if the question
is, all right,

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:58.430
there are lots of metrics, what
should we really pay attention

00:08:58.430 --> 00:08:59.300
to?

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:02.960
Fundamentally, there are really
three; traffic, how much stuff

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:06.770
is happening; latency, how
fast stuff is happening;

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:09.620
and errors, are our
users actually able to do

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:10.777
what they need to do.

00:09:10.777 --> 00:09:12.860
If you can add saturation
to that, which is really

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:15.140
just a way to
understand, basically,

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:18.463
our ability to deliver against
our capacity, that's great.

00:09:18.463 --> 00:09:19.880
Because that's the
first place you

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:22.412
can look at to
understand whether or not

00:09:22.412 --> 00:09:23.870
issues with your
service have to do

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:25.700
with running out of capacity.

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:27.680
But fundamentally,
that's really what

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:30.630
you need in order to understand
the health of your system.

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:33.650
So how do you get
metrics in Stackdriver?

00:09:33.650 --> 00:09:35.070
Where do they come from?

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:37.460
Well, if you're running
on NGCP, if you're easy

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:40.010
managed GCP resources,
and all of that stuff

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:42.118
is an instrument for you
right out of the box.

00:09:42.118 --> 00:09:44.660
So if you go to Stackdriver,
you go to the resources section,

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:47.190
there's an entire GCP area
that shows you the metrics

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:49.910
for all the various
GCP managed services.

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:51.920
So for example,
something like Pub/Sub,

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:54.128
it builds your dashboard
out of the box.

00:09:54.128 --> 00:09:54.920
You can see metric.

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:57.717
You can see information
all right in there.

00:09:57.717 --> 00:09:59.300
It allows to create
a learning policy.

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:01.893
If you need it, again,
it's Pub/Sub resources.

00:10:01.893 --> 00:10:03.560
You can obviously
filter the information

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:05.930
by a specific resource if
you want to filter it down

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:08.217
to a specific Pub/Sub topic.

00:10:08.217 --> 00:10:09.800
The other thing you
should be aware of

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:13.640
is this idea of
transparent SLIs.

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:16.240
This is a relatively new
addition to Stackdriver.

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:19.910
But it actually allows
you to include SLI signals

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:23.330
from the platform in your
own reporting and alerting,

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:24.820
even if you so choose.

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:28.490
So transparent SLIs give
you latency, error rate,

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:32.390
and traffic data for
Google Platform APIs.

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:35.150
So if you want to see like what
the latency is of the Compute

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:38.330
API within you're
within your project

00:10:38.330 --> 00:10:41.090
or any of the other APIs that
you're actually relying on,

00:10:41.090 --> 00:10:44.480
you can now include these
signals in your own dashboards,

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:47.150
correlate them with signals
that are being emitted

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:49.190
from your own
applications or services,

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:51.680
and really quickly understand
whether a problem that you're

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:54.583
observing has to do with your
code or something on our side.

00:10:54.583 --> 00:10:56.000
ANTHONY BUSHONG:
And so this might

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:57.963
be really useful
in scenarios where

00:10:57.963 --> 00:10:59.630
we're adopting a
mutable infrastructure,

00:10:59.630 --> 00:11:01.850
rolling out new changes
that is dependent upon some

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:02.912
of these platform APIs.

00:11:02.912 --> 00:11:05.120
And so we want to look at
that in kind of conjunction

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:05.810
with our environment.

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:07.685
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Exactly,
yeah, very much so.

00:11:10.893 --> 00:11:12.685
If you're running a
computer infrastructure

00:11:12.685 --> 00:11:14.380
in GCP, specifically,
[INAUDIBLE]

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:16.840
Compute Engine Instances,
metrics from those

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:19.300
are created automatically
when you first just spin up

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:20.380
the instances.

00:11:20.380 --> 00:11:22.750
You get basic metrics
like CPU, disk IO,

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:26.170
and network throughput,
just, again, out of the box,

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:27.400
automatically.

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:30.010
You also have the option of
installing the Stackdriver

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:32.380
Monitoring Agent
on those instances

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:34.330
to both capture
additional information,

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:36.430
like memory, for example.

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:38.590
Lots of additional
metrics also get created

00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:40.310
when you install the agent.

00:11:40.310 --> 00:11:42.550
And finally, you get a
process-level monitoring.

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:43.540
You can actually
see whether or not

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:45.082
a particular process
on your instance

00:11:45.082 --> 00:11:47.620
is running, how much
resources it's consuming.

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:49.810
And then you can create you
know alerting and things

00:11:49.810 --> 00:11:52.730
like that against that process,
as well, if you so choose.

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:56.870
So there's a lot of value to
using the monitoring agent.

00:11:56.870 --> 00:11:59.602
And finally, just a kind of
quick dive into custom metrics.

00:11:59.602 --> 00:12:01.060
Let's right let's
say that you want

00:12:01.060 --> 00:12:05.030
to emit specific information
from within your application.

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:06.595
You have a few options there.

00:12:06.595 --> 00:12:09.220
Your first option is to actually
use the Stackdriver Monitoring

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:11.320
API directly.

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:14.340
The big advantage of doing that
is that gives you full control.

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:16.810
You control exactly against
which resources the metrics

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:19.960
were written against, how
often they're written,

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:22.630
what the labels are,
all that sort of thing.

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:24.760
The big trade off,
obviously, is complexity.

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:27.690
You have to manage
all of that stuff.

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:30.300
Your second option
is to use OpenCensus,

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:32.790
which is a library that
does both distributed

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:35.400
tracing and the metric
instrumentation for a subset

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:37.042
of languages that it supports.

00:12:37.042 --> 00:12:38.250
And that's the big advantage.

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:39.792
You can actually
use a single library

00:12:39.792 --> 00:12:43.170
to do both tracing and
metric instrumentation.

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:46.020
The big sort of trade there
is that the language support

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:47.040
is a little bit uneven.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:49.193
Not every language that
opens has the support.

00:12:49.193 --> 00:12:50.610
Supports, today,
will actually let

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:52.240
you do both traces the metrics.

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:55.500
So you kind of have to
look at what are you

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:58.465
writing your application
in and what can you do.

00:12:58.465 --> 00:13:00.090
And then the last
option is Prometheus.

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:02.670
So this is an open-source
monitoring tool.

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:04.170
Lots of you may be
familiar with it.

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:08.243
I saw someone here earlier
with Prometheus shirt on.

00:13:08.243 --> 00:13:09.660
It gives you
additional visibility

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:13.860
into the infrastructure that
the Prometheus, basically,

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:16.050
instrumentation is running on.

00:13:16.050 --> 00:13:17.490
The big off there
is that, if you

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:20.220
want Prometheus metrics to
show up and Stackdriver,

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:23.760
the only way that works today
is if you have Prometheus

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:24.960
running in Kubernetes.

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:26.970
So if you install it
on the GKE cluster,

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:30.090
and then patch that deployment
with a Stackdriver collector,

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:34.820
you can ingest those
metrics into Stackdriver.

00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:36.980
So the last part
about Prometheus

00:13:36.980 --> 00:13:39.590
is that it allows
you to ingest--

00:13:39.590 --> 00:13:42.798
you can ingest Prometheus
metrics into Stackdriver.

00:13:42.798 --> 00:13:44.840
But the only way you can
do that is if Prometheus

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:46.382
is running in Kubernetes.

00:13:46.382 --> 00:13:48.590
Because then you can patch
that Prometheus deployment

00:13:48.590 --> 00:13:51.350
with a Stackdriver Collector
and ingest those metrics

00:13:51.350 --> 00:13:53.930
into Stackdriver.

00:13:53.930 --> 00:14:00.260
So the second kind of signals
we're talking about is logs.

00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:04.220
So logs are very much
events, what happened

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:07.310
at a specific point in time.

00:14:07.310 --> 00:14:09.030
They can be structured.

00:14:09.030 --> 00:14:11.780
They can be very unstructured,
as we've learned when

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:13.070
we try to build some demos.

00:14:13.070 --> 00:14:14.690
Sometimes you get
a log entry that's

00:14:14.690 --> 00:14:16.410
just one massive line of text.

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:18.540
And then you don't know
what to do with it.

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:20.498
So there's lots of places
where they come from.

00:14:20.498 --> 00:14:22.760
There's lots of data to store.

00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:26.120
There are a few different ways
to get logs into Stackdriver.

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:29.780
Logging, obviously all
of the GCP services emit

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:31.250
logs natively.

00:14:31.250 --> 00:14:33.080
There's nothing you
need to do there.

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:35.090
There's no setup at all.

00:14:35.090 --> 00:14:37.460
For your services, we
have client libraries

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:40.130
force Stackdriver logging
that you can use to, again,

00:14:40.130 --> 00:14:42.230
send logs to Stackdriver.

00:14:42.230 --> 00:14:44.630
And then, as you're
running on Kubernetes,

00:14:44.630 --> 00:14:46.550
you can use Fluentd
to basically just

00:14:46.550 --> 00:14:49.967
have your containers write logs
to Standard Out or Stand Error.

00:14:49.967 --> 00:14:51.800
And then Fluentd will
just ingest those logs

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:54.980
and send them to the
Stackdriver backend.

00:14:54.980 --> 00:14:59.120
And finally, traces, so
traces are all about latency,

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:01.250
especially understanding
service-to-service

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:04.100
communication latency
in distributed systems.

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:05.880
So this is
highly-structured data.

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:08.840
They're made up of
spans and child spans.

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:12.800
One trace is scoped
to a specific request.

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:14.420
And a few examples
of how you use

00:15:14.420 --> 00:15:17.000
tracing to get this
data into Stackdriver,

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:19.455
so the first option is you
can use the Trace Agent.

00:15:19.455 --> 00:15:21.080
So this is something
you literally just

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:22.130
include in your code.

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:24.920
You put it in as the very
sort of the top of the file.

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:27.350
And it'll trace all
the requests in and out

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:29.480
of that particular service.

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:31.730
The big advantage of using
the Stackdriver Trace Agent

00:15:31.730 --> 00:15:35.540
is that it's really easy,
literally one line of code.

00:15:35.540 --> 00:15:37.670
The big trade off of
using it is that it

00:15:37.670 --> 00:15:39.290
doesn't give you any control.

00:15:39.290 --> 00:15:42.080
So you can't actually
manually create spans--

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:44.730
or you can't manually
create traces of spans.

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:46.700
It'll just do tracing for you.

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:48.830
Your second option
is, again, OpenCensus.

00:15:48.830 --> 00:15:50.435
So we talked about
at the beginning.

00:15:50.435 --> 00:15:52.780
It gives you a lot more control.

00:15:52.780 --> 00:15:55.100
The big trade off
there is complexity.

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:57.570
And then your third
option is OpenTracing,

00:15:57.570 --> 00:15:59.900
which is another
open-source tracing library.

00:15:59.900 --> 00:16:02.720
The trade off of OpenTracing
is that it only does tracing.

00:16:02.720 --> 00:16:04.710
It does not do both
tracing and metrics.

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:06.650
The way that OpenCensus does.

00:16:06.650 --> 00:16:07.250
However--

00:16:07.250 --> 00:16:08.958
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Yeah,
so how do people--

00:16:08.958 --> 00:16:11.410
I mean, it seems like there's
a couple distinct libraries,

00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:12.880
OpenCensus, OpenTracing.

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:15.180
How do you folks actually
navigate that space?

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:17.138
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah,
that's a great question.

00:16:17.138 --> 00:16:19.130
And the good news
there is that we're--

00:16:19.130 --> 00:16:20.660
not "we."

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:23.078
These two projects
are actually merging.

00:16:23.078 --> 00:16:24.620
So here's the link
to the Medium post

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:26.150
that you can see
in the deck here.

00:16:26.150 --> 00:16:28.550
And you can find this
on Medium pretty easily.

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:31.130
But OpenTracing and OpenCensus
are actually going to merge.

00:16:31.130 --> 00:16:33.920
So there will really be
just a single open-source

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:36.440
tracing library that
you'll be able to use.

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:38.882
I don't think this is
going to happen tomorrow.

00:16:38.882 --> 00:16:40.340
But this is very
much the direction

00:16:40.340 --> 00:16:42.240
of these two projects.

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:44.720
And I say this as a sort
of an outside observer.

00:16:44.720 --> 00:16:48.590
I'm not, myself, a member
of either these projects.

00:16:48.590 --> 00:16:51.270
All right, so to kind of wrap
up this particular section,

00:16:51.270 --> 00:16:52.830
the observability,
or the ability

00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:55.600
to understand what is
happening in your system

00:16:55.600 --> 00:16:58.840
is really the combination of
three major signals, metrics,

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:00.760
logs, and traces.

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:03.060
Stackdriver gives
you a common backend

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:04.687
to collect all that information.

00:17:04.687 --> 00:17:06.270
We've talked about
some of the options

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:07.880
that you could use to do so.

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:09.359
And, yeah, you should do that.

00:17:09.359 --> 00:17:11.880
It gives you much
good information

00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:13.650
that you can use
during incidents

00:17:13.650 --> 00:17:15.180
to do troubleshooting.

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:16.319
ANTHONY BUSHONG: And I
think, just anecdotally,

00:17:16.319 --> 00:17:18.277
from my perspective, some
of the exciting stuff

00:17:18.277 --> 00:17:21.937
about Stackdriver specifically
is seeing folks come in,

00:17:21.937 --> 00:17:23.520
standardizing on
Prometheus, and being

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:25.911
able to see that there
is that integration.

00:17:25.911 --> 00:17:27.369
But there's also,
on the flip side,

00:17:27.369 --> 00:17:30.180
a lot of native integration
just into those GCP resources,

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:32.700
almost a no-opt to set up.

00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:33.900
So--

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:34.350
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
All right, so now,

00:17:34.350 --> 00:17:35.340
let's talk about
a third principle,

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:36.548
which is release engineering.

00:17:36.548 --> 00:17:40.188
ANTHONY BUSHONG: All right, so
I'm going to use this switch.

00:17:40.188 --> 00:17:41.730
It should look the
same if it worked.

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:42.015
YURI GRINSHTEYN: There it is.

00:17:42.015 --> 00:17:43.223
ANTHONY BUSHONG: There it is.

00:17:43.223 --> 00:17:44.040
All right, cool.

00:17:44.040 --> 00:17:49.470
So the last SRE principle that
we're going to cover today

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:50.620
is release engineering.

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:52.620
And so I just want to get
a quick show of hands.

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:55.437
For folks, when they think
about release engineering,

00:17:55.437 --> 00:17:57.270
does Stackdriver come
to mind, or monitoring

00:17:57.270 --> 00:18:00.230
in general come to mind,
as a forefront principal,

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:03.543
or a piece of
release engineering?

00:18:03.543 --> 00:18:04.460
Show of hands, anyone?

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:04.750
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
I'm just saying,

00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:06.292
I'm not putting my
hand for that one.

00:18:06.292 --> 00:18:07.800
ANTHONY BUSHONG: OK.

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:09.800
So, looks like we got
about four or five people.

00:18:09.800 --> 00:18:11.910
So looks like I have
some people to win over

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:14.070
as far as convincing
you all that Stackdriver

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:20.460
does play a very integral
role with release engineering

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:22.710
and, specifically, release
engineering in the way

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:26.640
that the SRE kind of
book defines it as.

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:28.980
So let's dive right into it.

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:31.080
So this is an actual
quote, so chapter 8.

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:33.300
For those of you who haven't
had the chance to comb

00:18:33.300 --> 00:18:36.390
through the SRE book, chapter
8 covers release engineering

00:18:36.390 --> 00:18:37.470
as a whole.

00:18:37.470 --> 00:18:40.650
And so I think this ties back to
the initial kind of discussion

00:18:40.650 --> 00:18:43.560
point that we had in that
first, second, or third slide,

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:47.720
talking about how, if we want
services to be more reliable,

00:18:47.720 --> 00:18:52.110
it requires to invest upfront in
a reliable and, in my opinion,

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:54.290
standardized release processes.

00:18:54.290 --> 00:18:57.300
It's essential that, when
we define these upfront,

00:18:57.300 --> 00:18:59.970
that developers, SREs,
and release engineers

00:18:59.970 --> 00:19:01.000
actually work together.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:04.110
So talking about breaking
down those boundaries,

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:08.580
oftentimes, is investing
upfront in kind of deciding

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:10.120
what that process looks like.

00:19:10.120 --> 00:19:12.450
And so a lot of that
has been kind of looped

00:19:12.450 --> 00:19:15.930
into a lot of the buzzwords
of continuous delivery,

00:19:15.930 --> 00:19:19.380
continuous CI/CD.

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:22.080
I think J. Paul Reed,
one of the DevOps days,

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:24.930
I think, in Atlanta, really
had my favorite definition

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:25.898
of continuous delivery.

00:19:25.898 --> 00:19:27.690
And that's getting your
entire organization

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:29.450
to care about
release engineering,

00:19:29.450 --> 00:19:32.018
and, I think, quality
assurance-- and he

00:19:32.018 --> 00:19:33.810
goes on to say-- in a
way that it had never

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:35.035
cared about them before.

00:19:35.035 --> 00:19:36.660
And so what we're
going to see here is,

00:19:36.660 --> 00:19:39.118
we're going to talk about what
release engineering actually

00:19:39.118 --> 00:19:40.480
means in the context of SRE.

00:19:40.480 --> 00:19:42.238
And then we're going
to talk about how

00:19:42.238 --> 00:19:44.280
quality assurance and
Stackdriver all kind of fit

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:46.680
into this picture.

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:49.170
so in chapter 8--

00:19:49.170 --> 00:19:51.123
who knew these four
principles by heart?

00:19:51.123 --> 00:19:51.730
No?

00:19:51.730 --> 00:19:52.230
OK.

00:19:52.230 --> 00:19:57.030
So we're going to talk about
what the SRE book kind of

00:19:57.030 --> 00:19:58.720
defines release engineering as.

00:19:58.720 --> 00:20:01.390
And it's really reliant
upon four key pillars.

00:20:01.390 --> 00:20:02.640
And that's self-service model.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:05.530
And I've tried to sum up in
kind of single-word summaries.

00:20:05.530 --> 00:20:10.620
So that's being able to provide
automation to your teams.

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:15.210
So as we know break out into
this microservices world,

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:18.810
into this two-pizza-team
world, each of these,

00:20:18.810 --> 00:20:21.360
without the investment in
release engineering upfront,

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:24.055
results in bespoke
processes that often--

00:20:24.055 --> 00:20:26.430
especially, I see it in folks
as they start to transition

00:20:26.430 --> 00:20:30.180
into Kubernetes, people fall
into the local optimum trap.

00:20:30.180 --> 00:20:32.193
Everyone is writing their
own little automation,

00:20:32.193 --> 00:20:33.360
fast scripts, what have you.

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:34.027
I'm guilty, too.

00:20:34.027 --> 00:20:36.708
For all my demos, I don't
always use the tools

00:20:36.708 --> 00:20:37.750
that I'm showing up here.

00:20:37.750 --> 00:20:39.370
Sometimes, I'll just right Bash.

00:20:39.370 --> 00:20:42.150
But ultimately, we want to
create a self-service model

00:20:42.150 --> 00:20:47.550
for folks and for teams
to be able to adopt.

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:51.630
And I think there's a lot of
that in the higher-level SRE

00:20:51.630 --> 00:20:55.080
principles of just
automating away toil.

00:20:55.080 --> 00:20:57.750
I won't touch, too much, on high
velocity and hermetic builds.

00:20:57.750 --> 00:20:59.820
But I think a lot
of this is being

00:20:59.820 --> 00:21:03.270
able to tie immutable
infrastructure in Docker.

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:06.060
Folks expecting to-- if
I have a Docker file,

00:21:06.060 --> 00:21:07.500
and have a
repository, this build

00:21:07.500 --> 00:21:09.300
should behave the
same way no matter

00:21:09.300 --> 00:21:10.440
where I'm running it from.

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:13.860
And also, just being able to,
rather than doing hot fixes,

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:15.840
and SSH-ing into the
machine, and just

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:16.890
trying to figure
out what's going on,

00:21:16.890 --> 00:21:19.230
and making those changes,
being able to just tear away

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:21.715
infrastructure, and deploy
a brand-new resource,

00:21:21.715 --> 00:21:23.840
and really treating my
infrastructure as immutable,

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:26.430
really tie in to these
middle-to-high velocity

00:21:26.430 --> 00:21:27.710
and hermetic builds pillars.

00:21:27.710 --> 00:21:29.460
But the last thing I'm
going to talk about

00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:32.010
is the enforcement of
policies and procedures.

00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:35.620
And so the I see this
is that a lot of folks--

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:38.520
and a lot of us get excited
about technology first.

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:40.680
And we get excited
about tools first.

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:43.650
But when it comes to the
pillars of release engineering,

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:48.780
oftentimes, we want to reflect
our own business processes

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:51.240
or compliance
processes that we're

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:54.027
held accountable for,
and the policies that

00:21:54.027 --> 00:21:56.610
are specific to our culture, to
the way that our team actually

00:21:56.610 --> 00:21:57.640
does things.

00:21:57.640 --> 00:22:01.000
And so I kind of sum that
up as configurability.

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:03.390
So the two pillars of
release engineering

00:22:03.390 --> 00:22:06.540
I'm going to focus in on is
automation and configurability.

00:22:06.540 --> 00:22:11.160
Now, so for folks who kind of
take these words at face value,

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:14.580
it kind of seems like they don't
have a whole bunch in common.

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:17.850
I think the SRE book talks
about automating away

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:20.670
releases to the point
where engineers are only

00:22:20.670 --> 00:22:22.080
needed if there are issues.

00:22:22.080 --> 00:22:25.860
But that seems a little
bit different from folks

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:28.620
adopting configurability
to match,

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:32.370
whether it's shipping off
to specific business units

00:22:32.370 --> 00:22:36.600
to perform kind of intense
tests or functional tests,

00:22:36.600 --> 00:22:39.750
or being able to
basically introduce

00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:43.720
these breakpoints where
automation is not possible,

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:46.000
so human approval processes.

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:49.130
Finding a sweet spot between
the two is often very difficult.

00:22:49.130 --> 00:22:54.690
So we're going to try and do
that in the next five minutes.

00:22:54.690 --> 00:22:59.560
So to solve that requires
an ops-centric, CI/CD kind

00:22:59.560 --> 00:23:00.530
of approach.

00:23:00.530 --> 00:23:03.700
So what I mean here is-- and
so here's just another warning.

00:23:03.700 --> 00:23:05.242
If every team is
figuring out they're

00:23:05.242 --> 00:23:06.658
own deployment
processes, you want

00:23:06.658 --> 00:23:08.190
to avoid that
local optimum trap.

00:23:08.190 --> 00:23:11.080
And we really want to
design for an ops-centric,

00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:12.760
continuous-delivery pipeline.

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:15.820
So this is the ability to
have a single pane of glass,

00:23:15.820 --> 00:23:17.590
to see how all of
your deployments

00:23:17.590 --> 00:23:21.040
for different services
are progressing, and being

00:23:21.040 --> 00:23:24.700
able to have that basic kind
of auditing trail of what

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:28.090
has been deployed, with
integrated monitoring.

00:23:28.090 --> 00:23:31.120
And so this does require a
bit more management effort

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:34.930
compared to some of the
other kind of methods that

00:23:34.930 --> 00:23:36.250
are outlined on this slide.

00:23:36.250 --> 00:23:38.050
But it really is
worth it to achieve

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:40.718
that balance between
configurability and automation.

00:23:40.718 --> 00:23:42.760
So let's actually take a
look at what that means.

00:23:42.760 --> 00:23:45.040
And the context that we're
setting for this today

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:46.820
is utilizing a tool
called Spinnaker.

00:23:46.820 --> 00:23:49.070
Can I get a show of hands
of who knows Spinnaker?

00:23:49.070 --> 00:23:49.570
OK.

00:23:49.570 --> 00:23:49.836
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Oh.

00:23:49.836 --> 00:23:49.970
Huh.

00:23:49.970 --> 00:23:51.387
ANTHONY BUSHONG:
Most of the room.

00:23:51.387 --> 00:23:54.070
All right, now we're wading
into familiar territory.

00:23:54.070 --> 00:23:56.320
And we'll also be talking
about how this fits together

00:23:56.320 --> 00:23:58.090
with Stackdriver.

00:23:58.090 --> 00:24:01.350
So Stackdriver, I think,
needs no introduction.

00:24:01.350 --> 00:24:05.350
We've covered it in depth,
provides a holistic solution

00:24:05.350 --> 00:24:06.670
across logging and monitoring.

00:24:06.670 --> 00:24:09.850
And Spinnaker is an open source
continuous delivery tool,

00:24:09.850 --> 00:24:11.920
again, really focused
on what happens

00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:14.170
once you have an
artifact, and how can we

00:24:14.170 --> 00:24:17.530
have Cloud-native APIs
to roll out things to VMs

00:24:17.530 --> 00:24:20.265
or to Kubernetes itself.

00:24:20.265 --> 00:24:21.890
So Spinnaker is an
open-source project.

00:24:21.890 --> 00:24:25.810
I think it initially
got its fame Netflix.

00:24:25.810 --> 00:24:30.380
But Google has contributed
to the project, as well.

00:24:30.380 --> 00:24:32.322
So Spinnaker-- if
you're not familiar

00:24:32.322 --> 00:24:33.780
with the basic
building blocks, I'm

00:24:33.780 --> 00:24:35.280
just going to quickly
touch on them,

00:24:35.280 --> 00:24:38.050
that we have the notion of
pipelines, stages, and tasks.

00:24:38.050 --> 00:24:41.500
So these are things
that can define--

00:24:41.500 --> 00:24:44.380
you'll see here in the sample
pipeline that we are triggered

00:24:44.380 --> 00:24:47.050
off of a new image,
or a new version

00:24:47.050 --> 00:24:49.330
of an image being pushed
to some type of registry.

00:24:49.330 --> 00:24:51.340
I think if you were to
do this within Google,

00:24:51.340 --> 00:24:53.380
you might set up
a Pub/Sub trigger

00:24:53.380 --> 00:24:56.050
once something is pushed out
to Google Container Registry.

00:24:56.050 --> 00:24:58.810
And then, from there, we may
have multiple different stages

00:24:58.810 --> 00:25:01.570
that define, let's say,
a canary deployment,

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:02.920
or something like that.

00:25:02.920 --> 00:25:06.710
So this pipeline is just
a sequence of actions.

00:25:06.710 --> 00:25:10.090
So let's start with the common
pattern here that I think--

00:25:10.090 --> 00:25:12.370
I've seen a lot of
customers that do experiment

00:25:12.370 --> 00:25:13.840
with Spinnaker kind of take on.

00:25:13.840 --> 00:25:15.880
And this is manual judgments.

00:25:15.880 --> 00:25:18.220
So this is the ability,
within our pipelines,

00:25:18.220 --> 00:25:23.770
to actually define a
human approval step.

00:25:23.770 --> 00:25:26.410
This might be something
where QA is monitoring

00:25:26.410 --> 00:25:29.560
in minutes or in hours.

00:25:29.560 --> 00:25:31.090
And they're looking
at a dashboard

00:25:31.090 --> 00:25:34.780
and to decide whether or not a
pipeline should move forward.

00:25:34.780 --> 00:25:39.100
So I think an example is
folks looking at observability

00:25:39.100 --> 00:25:42.430
across a canary deployment.

00:25:42.430 --> 00:25:44.380
But I think-- and
I believe this is

00:25:44.380 --> 00:25:47.080
a quote from
someone from Netflix

00:25:47.080 --> 00:25:49.240
who blogged about
manual judgments.

00:25:49.240 --> 00:25:51.110
And they talked
about, oftentimes,

00:25:51.110 --> 00:25:53.560
it is just a poor
developer staring

00:25:53.560 --> 00:25:56.520
at a whole bunch of
graphs in a dashboard,

00:25:56.520 --> 00:25:58.717
or combing through structured
or unstructured logs,

00:25:58.717 --> 00:26:00.550
and trying to figure
out whether they should

00:26:00.550 --> 00:26:02.160
move forward or roll back.

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:04.990
And so the challenge
with this is, not only

00:26:04.990 --> 00:26:07.210
do developers sometimes
find this boring--

00:26:07.210 --> 00:26:09.100
some developers, not all.

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:11.060
But this is also
challenging at scale.

00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:12.790
If we are deploying
more frequently,

00:26:12.790 --> 00:26:14.620
and we are introducing
more microservices

00:26:14.620 --> 00:26:20.263
into our environment, what
becomes the bottleneck?

00:26:20.263 --> 00:26:22.180
Does release engineering
become the bottleneck

00:26:22.180 --> 00:26:25.750
because every pipeline
requires someone

00:26:25.750 --> 00:26:27.400
to watch a dashboard
for an hour?

00:26:27.400 --> 00:26:30.370
At scale, it
becomes challenging.

00:26:30.370 --> 00:26:31.870
If you are doing
this, again-- and

00:26:31.870 --> 00:26:35.350
I'll talk about how we
would automate this away.

00:26:35.350 --> 00:26:38.000
I do want to show, quickly,
that folks still do this.

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:40.390
And I think for folks getting
started with pipelines,

00:26:40.390 --> 00:26:42.950
there's absolutely
nothing wrong with it.

00:26:42.950 --> 00:26:45.640
So to define kind of what I
was discussing in that slide,

00:26:45.640 --> 00:26:49.348
you'll see here that I have
an actual Spinnaker pipeline.

00:26:49.348 --> 00:26:51.640
And what we're looking at is
this manual approval stage

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:52.802
that I have here.

00:26:52.802 --> 00:26:54.760
So one of the things
that's really useful, even

00:26:54.760 --> 00:26:56.600
if you're not able to
introduce automation yet,

00:26:56.600 --> 00:26:58.392
or you're not running
into that scale issue

00:26:58.392 --> 00:27:03.040
yet, what you can do is begin
to kind of seed Stackdriver

00:27:03.040 --> 00:27:06.400
into this manual approval
process, or your release

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:07.167
engineering.

00:27:07.167 --> 00:27:09.250
So what we're able to do--
oh, actually, I already

00:27:09.250 --> 00:27:09.860
had that open.

00:27:09.860 --> 00:27:11.330
So let's just take a look at it.

00:27:11.330 --> 00:27:14.470
What we're actually able to
do is, within Metrics Explorer

00:27:14.470 --> 00:27:18.460
in Stackdriver Monitoring,
we can look at our metrics

00:27:18.460 --> 00:27:20.410
here, and actually
create filters,

00:27:20.410 --> 00:27:22.510
or group-specific
metrics, and actually

00:27:22.510 --> 00:27:24.070
create a dashboard with that.

00:27:24.070 --> 00:27:27.100
So a fairly easy win, if
you're utilizing Spinnaker,

00:27:27.100 --> 00:27:29.440
is to be able to
create custom charts

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:31.330
that you want folks
to be able to look

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:34.503
at for relevant metrics, to
help them make that decision,

00:27:34.503 --> 00:27:35.920
if they should
approve a pipeline,

00:27:35.920 --> 00:27:37.120
to move forward or not.

00:27:37.120 --> 00:27:39.250
So what you'll see
here is that I actually

00:27:39.250 --> 00:27:44.770
have this filter defined
for 500 Response Codes.

00:27:44.770 --> 00:27:47.740
And we see how, if I deploy
a baseline in the canary

00:27:47.740 --> 00:27:49.600
together, what
that actually looks

00:27:49.600 --> 00:27:52.880
like as far as the new version
with new configuration,

00:27:52.880 --> 00:27:55.780
and baseline, which is deployed
at the same time as canary

00:27:55.780 --> 00:27:56.680
to mimic production.

00:27:56.680 --> 00:27:58.180
And I don't want
to gloss over that.

00:27:58.180 --> 00:27:59.555
But we can't spend
too much time.

00:27:59.555 --> 00:28:03.460
The note is that it is helpful
to deploy a baseline if you are

00:28:03.460 --> 00:28:07.570
taking on canary deployments
so that you can have

00:28:07.570 --> 00:28:11.530
a fair comparison for the new
version of your application,

00:28:11.530 --> 00:28:13.150
or your canary, to production.

00:28:13.150 --> 00:28:14.650
And the reason that
that's important

00:28:14.650 --> 00:28:16.817
is because the production
environment that's already

00:28:16.817 --> 00:28:18.285
been deployed may already--

00:28:21.310 --> 00:28:23.668
they may have already filled
a cache from the database.

00:28:23.668 --> 00:28:25.210
And this is something
that a baseline

00:28:25.210 --> 00:28:27.860
would have to do from
scratch, much like a canary.

00:28:27.860 --> 00:28:28.300
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
So the first thing

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:29.900
you're doing, before you
even introduce really

00:28:29.900 --> 00:28:31.690
any automation, is
that you're basically

00:28:31.690 --> 00:28:35.460
making that decision of whether
to move forward or roll back

00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:36.070
standardized.

00:28:36.070 --> 00:28:36.520
ANTHONY BUSHONG: That's right.

00:28:36.520 --> 00:28:37.895
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
You were saying,

00:28:37.895 --> 00:28:40.360
instead of making a
subjective decision,

00:28:40.360 --> 00:28:41.350
look at this dashboard.

00:28:41.350 --> 00:28:42.660
Compare the thing.

00:28:42.660 --> 00:28:44.050
If it looks good, move forward.

00:28:44.050 --> 00:28:45.010
If not, look back.

00:28:45.010 --> 00:28:45.520
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Precisely.

00:28:45.520 --> 00:28:47.500
I think that Spinnaker
frames it in that way,

00:28:47.500 --> 00:28:49.780
to provide instructions
on this manual judgment.

00:28:49.780 --> 00:28:52.120
And because we can embed
a very simple link here,

00:28:52.120 --> 00:28:54.580
that we can create from
this Metrics Explorer chart,

00:28:54.580 --> 00:28:56.860
it's really useful
to provide context

00:28:56.860 --> 00:28:59.590
as far as what should be
observed for a specific service

00:28:59.590 --> 00:29:02.970
or application that's
being deployed.

00:29:02.970 --> 00:29:06.840
So that being said, if we
can introduce automation,

00:29:06.840 --> 00:29:07.790
we want to.

00:29:07.790 --> 00:29:10.230
So Spinnaker and Stackdriver,
as a combination,

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:12.340
allows for this, as well.

00:29:12.340 --> 00:29:14.900
So what you're looking
at is an actual--

00:29:14.900 --> 00:29:17.310
I should have noted that the
canary and bassline that I

00:29:17.310 --> 00:29:20.100
was presenting is deployed
into a Kubernetes environment.

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:21.600
And so you'll see
here, this is sort

00:29:21.600 --> 00:29:24.170
of what I was looking at
within that dashboard.

00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:26.220
The baseline, we have a canary.

00:29:26.220 --> 00:29:30.240
And both of these are actually
exporting Prometheus metrics

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:31.520
into Stackdriver.

00:29:31.520 --> 00:29:36.810
And then we are led to a
place called Canary Analysis

00:29:36.810 --> 00:29:38.530
and Kayenta in the diagram.

00:29:38.530 --> 00:29:39.510
And so what are these?

00:29:39.510 --> 00:29:41.343
So one thing you should
know about Spinnaker

00:29:41.343 --> 00:29:44.730
is that it is a micro
service-based platform.

00:29:44.730 --> 00:29:46.980
So Kayenta is one
of these services,

00:29:46.980 --> 00:29:49.500
that is part of Spinnaker,
that provides automated canary

00:29:49.500 --> 00:29:50.570
analysis.

00:29:50.570 --> 00:29:53.070
And so what this means is
that, if we have metrics,

00:29:53.070 --> 00:29:56.670
we can actually begin to create
automation where we define,

00:29:56.670 --> 00:29:58.740
based on these
metrics, based on what

00:29:58.740 --> 00:30:01.680
we're looking at for a
non-production, environment,

00:30:01.680 --> 00:30:02.710
can we get a score?

00:30:02.710 --> 00:30:05.820
And based on that score, should
that pipeline automatically

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:07.500
move forward?

00:30:07.500 --> 00:30:12.360
So this is where Kayenta and
Stackdriver come into play.

00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:15.738
Again, Kayenta is used to
automate this canary judgment.

00:30:15.738 --> 00:30:17.280
And you can introduce
this as a stage

00:30:17.280 --> 00:30:18.690
and you're Spinnaker pipeline.

00:30:18.690 --> 00:30:21.630
It doesn't involve a human
operator to judge the releases.

00:30:21.630 --> 00:30:23.760
We are distinctly taking
this from the metrics

00:30:23.760 --> 00:30:28.290
that we are collecting
within Stackdriver.

00:30:28.290 --> 00:30:31.403
So in the example today, we're
looking at the request metric

00:30:31.403 --> 00:30:32.070
from Prometheus.

00:30:32.070 --> 00:30:34.693
So if you are using
or standardizing

00:30:34.693 --> 00:30:37.110
on a set of metrics, if you
can get them into Stackdriver,

00:30:37.110 --> 00:30:41.340
you can build them into
this automated canary

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:43.260
analysis within your pipeline.

00:30:43.260 --> 00:30:47.280
The last thing I'll note is that
you can group together metrics

00:30:47.280 --> 00:30:50.250
and weight specific metrics if
there's several metrics that we

00:30:50.250 --> 00:30:51.030
need to look at.

00:30:51.030 --> 00:30:54.940
In my example, we have
a very simple example.

00:30:54.940 --> 00:30:56.940
But I want to note,
there is the ability

00:30:56.940 --> 00:30:59.280
to introduce complexity,
much like I'm sure you'll

00:30:59.280 --> 00:31:00.743
find in your services, as well.

00:31:00.743 --> 00:31:02.410
YURI GRINSHTEYN: And
I should point out,

00:31:02.410 --> 00:31:05.760
kind of referring back to our
first principle, talking SLOs,

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:08.130
with SLO monitoring, those
actually become available

00:31:08.130 --> 00:31:10.650
as metrics in Stackdriver.

00:31:10.650 --> 00:31:13.698
You can have Kayenta
use your SLO metrics,

00:31:13.698 --> 00:31:15.240
and not just things
like error rates.

00:31:15.240 --> 00:31:16.990
You can actually see
like what is my error

00:31:16.990 --> 00:31:18.887
rate over a 30 minute
rolling window,

00:31:18.887 --> 00:31:20.970
to be able to make those
judgments pretty quickly,

00:31:20.970 --> 00:31:24.120
and make those judgments
off the same judgments

00:31:24.120 --> 00:31:25.950
that you use to
determine whether or not

00:31:25.950 --> 00:31:27.300
your actual service is healthy.

00:31:27.300 --> 00:31:28.720
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Precisely.

00:31:28.720 --> 00:31:30.150
So what I'm showing
you here is we

00:31:30.150 --> 00:31:31.800
have that very same
pipeline, except,

00:31:31.800 --> 00:31:34.200
now, we actually have a
stage for canary analysis

00:31:34.200 --> 00:31:35.840
where there was once
manual judgment.

00:31:35.840 --> 00:31:37.590
And so what we see
here is, if we actually

00:31:37.590 --> 00:31:40.350
look at what is configured
within this canary analysis

00:31:40.350 --> 00:31:43.007
stage, is that we actually
are configuring metrics here.

00:31:43.007 --> 00:31:45.090
And so you'll see here
that I only have one group.

00:31:45.090 --> 00:31:45.810
So this is Group 1.

00:31:45.810 --> 00:31:48.060
But we can actually create
multiple groups of metrics

00:31:48.060 --> 00:31:50.310
and then assign each of
those a score as far as

00:31:50.310 --> 00:31:53.850
how those should be weighted
in this canary-analysis stage.

00:31:53.850 --> 00:31:55.380
So you'll see here
that, again, I'm

00:31:55.380 --> 00:31:59.165
going off of the actual error
code here, which is 500.

00:31:59.165 --> 00:32:00.540
If we see an
increase in that, we

00:32:00.540 --> 00:32:02.710
should expect the
pipeline to fail.

00:32:02.710 --> 00:32:07.080
And so what is really good about
that is that we can now, again,

00:32:07.080 --> 00:32:14.130
for Spinnaker pipelines
that don't kind of, I guess,

00:32:14.130 --> 00:32:17.400
yield any distinct, or
useful, or new information

00:32:17.400 --> 00:32:20.850
about your application
or your service,

00:32:20.850 --> 00:32:23.825
we now no longer have to insert
a human there to, let's say,

00:32:23.825 --> 00:32:25.950
monitor a dashboard for 30
minutes, or for an hour,

00:32:25.950 --> 00:32:26.880
to come back to it.

00:32:26.880 --> 00:32:29.100
And then, even from
there, to make a decision

00:32:29.100 --> 00:32:30.900
that isn't necessarily
standardized.

00:32:30.900 --> 00:32:33.270
Now, we can provide
automation where needed so

00:32:33.270 --> 00:32:34.890
that, if we do have
a set of metrics

00:32:34.890 --> 00:32:36.810
where we can define
this threshold,

00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:40.080
we can introduce this
using Spinnaker, Kayenta,

00:32:40.080 --> 00:32:43.045
and Stackdriver.

00:32:43.045 --> 00:32:47.860
So The last thing I want to
note is that, whether or not

00:32:47.860 --> 00:32:50.440
you're able to automate
this process, I think,

00:32:50.440 --> 00:32:52.180
is an open question
for a lot of folks.

00:32:52.180 --> 00:32:55.420
Again, and a lot of it will have
to match sort of what approval

00:32:55.420 --> 00:32:57.190
processes you require today.

00:32:57.190 --> 00:32:59.890
But regardless of whether
there's manual approval

00:32:59.890 --> 00:33:03.160
or automation, one thing I
think folks can adopt and get

00:33:03.160 --> 00:33:05.410
a quick win, as far as
Spinnaker and Stackdriver,

00:33:05.410 --> 00:33:07.210
is actually monitoring
with context,

00:33:07.210 --> 00:33:09.130
and what I mean by
that is actually

00:33:09.130 --> 00:33:13.060
being able to utilize the notion
of a counter for a Spinnaker

00:33:13.060 --> 00:33:16.240
deployments, or events from
Spinnaker, and overlay that

00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:18.260
on the performance
of your application.

00:33:18.260 --> 00:33:22.920
And so the way that we actually
have this, an example here.

00:33:22.920 --> 00:33:24.670
And actually let me
talk through it first.

00:33:24.670 --> 00:33:27.700
And we'll look at the demo.

00:33:27.700 --> 00:33:30.550
Orca is-- again, as I
mentioned, Spinnaker

00:33:30.550 --> 00:33:32.440
is based on a set
of microservices.

00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:34.060
So Orca is the workflow engine.

00:33:34.060 --> 00:33:37.360
And Orca actually
emits events to Echo

00:33:37.360 --> 00:33:43.210
so what we can actually do is
configure Echo to basically--

00:33:43.210 --> 00:33:47.930
we can provide an external URL
to actually get these events.

00:33:47.930 --> 00:33:51.080
So this might be a Spinnaker
pipeline being created.

00:33:51.080 --> 00:33:53.590
This might be a specific
stage being complete.

00:33:53.590 --> 00:33:56.020
We can actually use something
like Google Cloud Functions,

00:33:56.020 --> 00:33:57.850
or anything that has
an external endpoint,

00:33:57.850 --> 00:34:00.460
and actually write that into
Stackdriver using the Logging

00:34:00.460 --> 00:34:01.780
Client Library.

00:34:01.780 --> 00:34:06.370
So what we're able to do here
, is we can configure Echo

00:34:06.370 --> 00:34:10.433
in Spinnaker to basically write
these events to a Google com

00:34:10.433 --> 00:34:12.850
function, which will then write
that into that Stackdriver

00:34:12.850 --> 00:34:13.353
Logging.

00:34:13.353 --> 00:34:14.770
So it's actually,
look at that, we

00:34:14.770 --> 00:34:20.260
can see here that I have a
whole bunch of different logs

00:34:20.260 --> 00:34:22.750
in Stackdriver
Logging that actually

00:34:22.750 --> 00:34:26.230
signify some of these events
that Orca is emitting.

00:34:26.230 --> 00:34:27.989
And what we can
actually do here--

00:34:27.989 --> 00:34:29.989
and I haven't done this
in preparation for this.

00:34:29.989 --> 00:34:32.590
But we actually can create a
log-based metric that actually

00:34:32.590 --> 00:34:34.230
counts for something specific.

00:34:34.230 --> 00:34:36.130
So if a pipeline was
successful, let's

00:34:36.130 --> 00:34:39.100
actually create a
logs-based metric

00:34:39.100 --> 00:34:41.800
based on a filter for
that specific log.

00:34:41.800 --> 00:34:43.679
So this is something
where, now, we can then,

00:34:43.679 --> 00:34:45.889
in Metrics Explorer, if
we were to create this,

00:34:45.889 --> 00:34:48.460
create a dashboard that
provides that context of,

00:34:48.460 --> 00:34:51.030
this deployment happened, and
we're looking at all of these,

00:34:51.030 --> 00:34:52.780
let's say, error
response codes over time.

00:34:55.760 --> 00:35:00.055
So again, kind of marrying
the two concepts of automation

00:35:00.055 --> 00:35:02.443
and configurability,
definitely not an easy task.

00:35:02.443 --> 00:35:05.110
But I do believe that there is a
good story, if you're utilizing

00:35:05.110 --> 00:35:08.140
Spinnaker to integrate
Stackdriver within that,

00:35:08.140 --> 00:35:13.450
to implement the both of these
pillars of release engineering

00:35:13.450 --> 00:35:14.583
as defined by the SRE book.

00:35:14.583 --> 00:35:15.500
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah.

00:35:15.500 --> 00:35:17.690
So I think you showed us three
really interesting things

00:35:17.690 --> 00:35:18.190
there.

00:35:18.190 --> 00:35:21.352
The first one is just to
include Stackdriver charts

00:35:21.352 --> 00:35:22.810
in your Spinnaker
manual judgments.

00:35:22.810 --> 00:35:23.650
ANTHONY BUSHONG:
An opinion on that.

00:35:23.650 --> 00:35:25.358
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah,
it's super useful.

00:35:25.358 --> 00:35:28.360
Because it actually gives folks
a sort of a standardized way

00:35:28.360 --> 00:35:29.510
to make those judgments.

00:35:29.510 --> 00:35:29.740
ANTHONY BUSHONG: That's right.

00:35:29.740 --> 00:35:30.310
YURI GRINSHTEYN: And
then you showed us

00:35:30.310 --> 00:35:32.570
how to actually do
automation off those metrics.

00:35:32.570 --> 00:35:34.380
So have, that pipeline
make the decision,

00:35:34.380 --> 00:35:36.100
or have Spinnaker
make that decision

00:35:36.100 --> 00:35:37.640
based on those metrics.

00:35:37.640 --> 00:35:39.130
And finally,
actually use the data

00:35:39.130 --> 00:35:41.980
that's coming out of Spinnaker
to inform you even better,

00:35:41.980 --> 00:35:44.320
to really understand how
performance or availability is

00:35:44.320 --> 00:35:46.300
impacted by deployment events.

00:35:46.300 --> 00:35:46.810
ANTHONY BUSHONG: That's right.

00:35:46.810 --> 00:35:48.185
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
All right, cool.

00:35:48.185 --> 00:35:51.230
So let's review the three
principles we've covered today.

00:35:51.230 --> 00:35:53.915
We started out by talking
about error budgets and SLOs.

00:35:53.915 --> 00:35:55.540
And kind of the big
takeaway is, there,

00:35:55.540 --> 00:35:57.550
that, one, you should use them.

00:35:57.550 --> 00:35:58.630
They're really good.

00:35:58.630 --> 00:36:00.760
And two, is that
Stackdriver now,

00:36:00.760 --> 00:36:04.002
with the announcement of
Anthos and the Anthos Service

00:36:04.002 --> 00:36:05.710
Mesh this week, allows
you to actually do

00:36:05.710 --> 00:36:07.258
proper SLO monitoring.

00:36:07.258 --> 00:36:09.300
So you should absolutely
configure SLO monitoring

00:36:09.300 --> 00:36:10.833
in Stackdriver.

00:36:10.833 --> 00:36:12.250
Then we talked
about observability

00:36:12.250 --> 00:36:14.583
and how to use Stackdriver
to collect the golden signals

00:36:14.583 --> 00:36:16.360
from your services
and combine them

00:36:16.360 --> 00:36:18.610
into a single
place for analysis.

00:36:18.610 --> 00:36:20.860
And then, finally, we talked
about actually including

00:36:20.860 --> 00:36:23.260
Stackdriver data as an input
in your release engineering

00:36:23.260 --> 00:36:26.680
practices, specifically, how do
you evaluate canary judgments

00:36:26.680 --> 00:36:28.180
using Stackdriver metrics.

00:36:28.180 --> 00:36:32.430
